<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narni</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Narnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narni is a hill town in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, above a gorge of the Nera river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> founded the colony of Narnia on the site of the Umbrian town of Nequinum in 299 BCE to guard the routes into Umbria, and the Via Flaminia later crossed the Nera there on the Bridge of Augustus, whose ruins survive. The Roman emperor Nerva was born there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narni.jpg" alt="Narni" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikiriello · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narni_con_Drone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Narni stands on a rock 240 metres above sea level on the left bank of the Nera, about 12 kilometres from the river's confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>. The town overlooks a narrow gorge of the Nera and the Terni valley, near the historical boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a>, 14 kilometres from Terni and 82 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It lies very close to the geographical centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, marked by a stone with a sign in several languages. Its ancient name Narnia comes from the river, called the Nera or Nar, and before the Roman conquest the town appears to have been called Nequinum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Narni was an important Umbrian city. In 300 BCE the consul Appuleius besieged it, and the inhabitants resisted so vigorously that the siege continued into the next year, when the consul Marcus Fulvius took the city and held a triumph over the Samnites and the people of Nequinum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> secured the conquest by establishing a colony. Its position above the Via Flaminia gave it importance in the wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and in the late 6th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a> and the Lombard Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> contested it, since it controlled the southern part of the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and Ravenna.</p><p>A turning point came in 1527, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsknecht">Landsknechts</a> returning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> besieged and looted it, with Terni assisting; about 1530 it was found largely deserted. Popes Paul III and Pius IV confirmed tax breaks to rebuild the walls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>, but a plague in 1591 weakened it again. The town came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">Napoleonic Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> visited in 1800 and in May 1804.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Among the surviving Roman works is the Ponte d'Augusto, one of the largest Roman bridges ever built, by which the Via Flaminia crossed the Nera; one arch still stands. The Roman aqueduct of the Formina, about 13 kilometres long, was built in the 1st century CE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and supplied the town until 1924. The Rocca Albornoziana is a square fortress with four corner towers, part of the papal system of fortresses set up after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. The cathedral is dedicated to Saint Juvenal of Narni, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. C. S. Lewis named the land of Narnia after the town, having come across its name in an atlas as a child.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome founded <b>Narnia</b> in 299 BCE, and after the battle of Sentinum in 295 BCE, where some Umbrians backed the anti-Roman coalition, the Umbrian towns became allies.</blockquote><a href="/state/umbrians">Umbrians</a></li></ul></section>
